scenarios.prompt:
I have this MobX action:

```ts
async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  const items = await this.service.getAll();
  this.items = items;
  this.loading = false;
}
```

What needs to change so it works correctly under MobX's enforceActions?

trialRecords.output:
Two things are wrong with this action under `enforceActions`: the mutations after the `await` happen outside any action context, and — less obviously — `this.loading = true` before the `await` is fine, but the two post-await assignments (`this.items = items` and `this.loading = false`) are the ones that will throw.

Here's what the fix looks like, following the store shape in this project:

```ts
@action.bound
async fetchItems(): Promise<void> {
  this.loading = true;
  const items = await this.service.getAll();
  runInAction(() => {
    this.items = items;
    this.loading = false;
  });
}
```

**Why only the post-await lines move inside `runInAction`:**

`await` suspends the action's execution context. When the function resumes, everything after the `await` runs in a fresh microtask — a *new* execution context that is no longer wrapped in the original `@action`. So `this.loading = true` (before the `await`) is still inside the action and is fine, but both assignments after the `await` are now outside it and will throw `[MobX] Since strict-mode is enabled, changing (observed) observable values without using an action is not allowed`.

**If the guard is important, add it:**

```ts
@action.bound
async fetchItems(): Promise<void> {
  if (this.loading) return;
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = await this.service.getAll();
    runInAction(() => {
      this.items = items;
      this.loading = false;
    });
  } catch (err) {
    runInAction(() => {
      this.loading = false;
    });
    throw err;
  }
}
```

Without the `try/catch`, a rejected `getAll()` leaves `loading` stuck at `true` forever, because the `runInAction` that clears it never runs.

**Two alternatives worth knowing about:**

1. **`flow`** — if this store has *many* sequential-await actions and the repeated `runInAction` blocks are the dominant noise in the file, convert the action to a generator:

   ```ts
   fetchItems = flow(function* (this: ItemsStore) {
     this.loading = true;
     try {
       const items = yield this.service.getAll();
       this.items = items;
       this.loading = false;
     } catch (err) {
       this.loading = false;
       throw err;
     }
   });
   ```

   `flow` wraps the generator so every `yield` resume happens inside an action automatically — no manual `runInAction`. Note that `flow` returns a function you assign as a property (not a method you decorate), and you need `this` typed explicitly. Use this only if the `runInAction` repetition is genuinely a problem; for a single action like this one, the `runInAction` version is clearer and matches the project's existing pattern.

2. **Don't reach for `makeAutoObservable` to "fix" this** — it makes the method an action automatically, but it does **not** make the post-await mutations legal. The suspension problem is identical. You still need `runInAction` or `flow`.

**What NOT to do:**

- Don't disable `enforceActions` to make it pass. The configure call usually lives in an app-entry or test-setup file, and turning off enforcement lets exactly this class of bug through silently — mutations that fire outside the reactivity batch and cause missed/duplicated re-renders that are far harder to debug than the thrown error.
- Don't move *all* three assignments into a single `runInAction`, including `this.loading = true`. That doesn't work at all — the `await` is in the middle, so you can't have one `runInAction` straddle it.

One more thing to verify while you're here: make sure `this.items` is actually declared observable (e.g. `@observable items: Item[] = []`) and that the class is set up with `makeObservable(this)` in the constructor. If `items` isn't observable, the assignment won't throw but also won't notify any observer — which is the more insidious version of this same bug.

Under `enforceActions`, the two mutations after the `await` (`this.items = items` and `this.loading = false`) happen *outside* the action's synchronous run, so MobX will throw (in `"always"`/`"observed"` config) or warn. Only `this.loading = true` is safe, because it runs synchronously before the first suspension point.

The fix, following the project's async-action shape (`rules/patterns.mdc`):

```ts
async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  const items = await this.service.getAll();
  runInAction(() => {
    this.items = items;
    this.loading = false;
  });
}
```

Why this works: everything before the `await` still executes inside the action's synchronous run, so `this.loading = true` is fine. Everything after the `await` resumes on a later tick — a new, non-action context — so it must be re-wrapped in `runInAction`.

Points to note:

- If this is a public, UI-invoked method, prefer `@action.bound` on `fetchItems` and keep the real work in a `private async` method, so the public surface stays thin and the bound action keeps `this` correct when passed as a callback.
- If the store has several sequential-await actions and the repeated `runInAction` blocks become dominant noise, that's the case to switch that store to `flow` generators instead — but for a single action like this, `runInAction` is the right call.
- Don't rely on `enforceActions` being off as a reason to skip the wrapping; it will break the moment the config is tightened or the store is used in a test suite that enables it.
- The error path matters too: if `this.service.getAll()` rejects, the `runInAction` never runs and `loading` stays `true` forever. Wrap the await in `try`/`catch`/`finally` and reset `loading` inside `finally` (still within `runInAction` if it's post-await).

After the change, verify: type-check passes, every post-`await` mutation is inside `runInAction` (or the method is a `flow`), and any component reading `items`/`loading` is wrapped in `observer` so it actually re-renders.

Under `enforceActions: "always"` (or `"observed"`), the assignments after the `await` throw — the async continuation runs outside the action's synchronous context, so the action wrapper has already returned by the time `this.items = items` executes. Only `this.loading = true` (which runs synchronously before the first `await`) is still inside the action.

You have two options. The one your project's `rules/patterns.mdc` prescribes is the thin-public-action + private-worker + `runInAction` shape:

```ts
@action.bound
async fetchItems(): Promise<void> {
  await this.runFetchItems();
}

@action
private async runFetchItems(): Promise<void> {
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = await this.service.getAll();
    runInAction(() => {
      this.items = items;
      this.loading = false;
    });
  } catch (error) {
    runInAction(() => {
      this.loading = false;
    });
    throw error;
  }
}
```

Key points:

- `runInAction` wraps each group of post-`await` mutations, so nothing after an `await` relies on the action context surviving.
- The public method stays thin (guard + delegate); the real work lives in the `private async` worker. This matches the async-action pattern in `rules/patterns.mdc`.
- `this.loading = false` belongs in a `finally` or a `catch` too — if `getAll()` rejects, the original code leaves `loading` stuck at `true` forever. Don't drop that.
- Note that the synchronous `this.loading = true` before the first `await` is fine either way; you can leave it in the worker or lift it into the public action. Keeping it in the worker with the rest of the loading logic reads more consistently.

The alternative is `flow`, if this store has many sequential-await actions and the repeated `runInAction` blocks are the dominant noise:

```ts
fetchItems = flow(function* (this: typeof Store.prototype) {
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = yield this.service.getAll();
    this.items = items;
    this.loading = false;
  } catch (error) {
    this.loading = false;
    throw error;
  }
});
```

`flow` keeps each `yield` resumption inside the action context, so no `runInAction` is needed. Per `rules/patterns.mdc`, reach for `flow` only when it genuinely reduces noise across the file — for a single action like this, the `runInAction` shape is the project default.

Either way, verify with the project's type-check and a test that asserts `items` and `loading` update correctly, including the rejection path.

Under `enforceActions` (strict mode), this action is broken in two places: the assignments after the `await`.

**Why it fails:** `await` splits the function into two halves. The synchronous half — `this.loading = true` — runs *inside* the action's transaction, so it's fine. But everything after `await` resumes in a *new microtask*, outside the original action context. So `this.items = items` and `this.loading = false` are untracked mutations, and under `enforceActions: "always"` they throw `[MobX] Since strict-mode is enabled, changing (observed) observable values without using an action is not allowed`.

**The fix — wrap the post-await mutations in `runInAction`:**

```ts
import { runInAction } from "mobx";

async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  const items = await this.service.getAll();
  runInAction(() => {
    this.items = items;
    this.loading = false;
  });
}
```

Also add error handling, or a failed fetch leaves `loading` stuck at `true` forever:

```ts
async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = await this.service.getAll();
    runInAction(() => {
      this.items = items;
      this.loading = false;
    });
  } catch (error) {
    runInAction(() => {
      this.loading = false;
    });
    throw error; // or set this.error = error, per project convention
  }
}
```

**The project-convention shape:** per this skill's async-action pattern, a *public* action should stay thin — guard check, then delegate to a private worker that does the real work. So:

```ts
@action.bound
async fetchItems() {
  if (this.loading) return;
  await this.loadItems();
}

private async loadItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = await this.service.getAll();
    runInAction(() => {
      this.items = items;
      this.loading = false;
    });
  } catch (error) {
    runInAction(() => { this.loading = false; });
    throw error;
  }
}
```

**Alternative — `flow`:** only reach for this if the store has many sequential-await actions and the repeated `runInAction` blocks are genuinely the dominant noise:

```ts
fetchItems = flow(function* (this: ItemStore) {
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = yield this.service.getAll();
    this.items = items;
    this.loading = false;
  } finally {
    this.loading = false;
  }
});
```

`flow` keeps the whole generator inside an action context automatically, so no `runInAction` is needed — but it's a bigger style change, so match whatever the existing store already does.

**Verification before calling it done:** type-check passes; every post-`await` mutation is inside `runInAction` (or is a `flow`); test that a rejected `service.getAll()` still resets `loading` to `false`.

The two post-`await` assignments (`this.items = items` and `this.loading = false`) run outside the action's synchronous body, so under `enforceActions: "always"` they'll throw (or warn) as mutations outside an action. Wrap them in `runInAction`:

```ts
import { observable, action, runInAction } from "mobx";

async fetchItems() {
  const service = this.service;
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = await service.getAll();
    runInAction(() => {
      this.items = items;
      this.loading = false;
    });
  } catch (err) {
    runInAction(() => {
      this.loading = false;
    });
    throw err;
  }
}
```

Notes on why each change matters:

- **`runInAction` around `this.items = items`** — the assignment happens after the `await`, so it's no longer part of the action's synchronous execution. `runInAction` starts a fresh action for the mutation, satisfying `enforceActions`.
- **`runInAction` around `this.loading = false`** — same reason; without it the store is left with `loading: true` (or the mutation throws) after the fetch resolves.
- **`try`/`catch` with a `loading = false` in the catch** — without it, a rejected `this.service.getAll()` leaves `loading` stuck `true` forever, and the error propagates unhandled. If your project's pattern is to surface the error as observable state instead of rethrowing, use that instead, but the reset still needs to run.
- **`this.loading = true` at the top stays as-is** — it's synchronous, so as long as the method is annotated `@action.bound` (or the method is called within an action), it's fine. If `fetchItems` might be invoked as a bare callback (e.g. passed to a button's `onClick`), make it `@action.bound` so its `this` and action context survive.

If this store has many actions with the same sequential-await shape, the alternative to repeated `runInAction` blocks is `flow`:

```ts
fetchItems = flow(function* (this: XyzStore) {
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    this.items = yield this.service.getAll();
  } finally {
    this.loading = false;
  }
});
```

`flow` turns each `yield`-resumption into an action automatically, so no `runInAction` wrappers are needed. Reach for it only when the repeated `runInAction` calls are genuinely the dominant noise in the file — for a single action like this one, `runInAction` is the clearer choice.

Also worth confirming separate from `enforceActions`: `this.service` should be a `private readonly` constructor parameter, not observable, so it doesn't participate in the reactivity graph.

